1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,700
Raja Ravi Varma painted the Indian epics in a European oil style.

2
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:07,450
What if he could paint any scene you describe, in a pose you choose?

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:09,950
This is The Next Ravi Varma, by Team The Unsupervised.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,450
You type a scene from the Mahabharata or Ramayana,

5
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:14,950
optionally add a photo of someone posing,

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,283
and get a new painting in his oil style.

7
00:00:18,333 --> 00:00:21,283
Our project follows the four milestones of our proposal:

8
00:00:21,333 --> 00:00:26,617
the data, the style, the words, and the pose, all inside one web app.

9
00:00:26,667 --> 00:00:31,617
Milestone one: the data. We collected 135 images of his work from Wikimedia Commons.

10
00:00:31,667 --> 00:00:37,450
Printed copies, called oleographs, look flat, so we kept only his 61 oil paintings and held nine out for testing.

11
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:41,617
Older captioning models wrote poor labels, so Qwen3-VL described every painting,

12
00:00:41,667 --> 00:00:44,950
and each caption starts with our trigger phrase: rrvarma oil painting.

13
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,783
Milestone two: the style. We trained a three-megabyte LoRA on frozen Stable Diffusion 1.5.

14
00:00:50,833 --> 00:00:55,783
Plain Stable Diffusion paints a photo, and asking for Ravi Varma gives a calendar print.

15
00:00:55,833 --> 00:01:03,283
Our LoRA paints in oil: style similarity 0.82, close to 0.84 for his real paintings.

16
00:01:03,333 --> 00:01:08,283
Milestone three: the words. Qwen 2.5 expands a short scene into a detailed prompt.

17
00:01:08,333 --> 00:01:11,617
Then four paintings are made, and a CLIP judge keeps the best one.

18
00:01:11,667 --> 00:01:14,950
That raised scene match from 28.2 to 30.4.

19
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,283
Milestone four: the pose. OpenPose turns a photo into a skeleton,

20
00:01:18,333 --> 00:01:22,033
and ControlNet paints a figure that follows it.

21
00:01:22,083 --> 00:01:26,617
We also describe each character, check the pose of every candidate, and refine at high resolution.

22
00:01:26,667 --> 00:01:29,950
With text alone, the model gets poses wrong and adds extra people.

23
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,283
With a pose photo, pose accuracy jumps from 10 to 97 percent,

24
00:01:33,333 --> 00:01:36,617
with no loss in how well the painting matches the scene.

25
00:01:36,667 --> 00:01:41,617
Here is our web app. I type a scene and press Paint.

26
00:01:41,667 --> 00:01:47,450
Four paintings are made and the best is kept; the prompt and seed are shown below.

27
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:54,950
Fast mode gives a quick single-image preview.

28
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,783
Now I upload a photo of someone drawing a bow and press Paint.

29
00:02:00,833 --> 00:02:04,950
The app shows the skeleton and paints Chitrangada in exactly that pose.

30
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,283
All of this runs on a free Colab T4 GPU.

31
00:02:08,333 --> 00:02:12,450
His style, any scene, any pose.

32
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:18,283
Our code, data and evaluations are public on GitHub. Thank you.

